
1. Thread-Local Storage (The Imperative Paradigm)

In traditional, synchronous frameworks where one thread handles one request from start to finish (e.g., Spring Boot with Spring MVC, Java Servlets), 
Thread-Local Storage is the standard approach.
• How it works: Data is stored in a thread-specific global map. 
Because the execution flows entirely on a single thread 
from the Controller through the Service to the DAO, any layer can fetch the data from the thread context.

• Common implementations:
	• Java's ThreadLocal or InheritableThreadLocal
	• Spring's RequestContextHolder or SecurityContextHolder
	• Python's threading.local


